    'sha224-hash-generator' => [
        'content' => '<h2>Everything You Need to Know About SHA-224 Hashing</h2><p>The <strong>SHA-224 (Secure Hash Algorithm 224)</strong> is a cryptographic hash function that is part of the SHA-2 family. While often overshadowed by its more famous sibling, SHA-256, SHA-224 is a vital tool for developers requiring a specific bit-length for their data integrity checks. It produces a 224-bit (28-byte) hash value, typically represented as a 56-digit hexadecimal number.</p><h3>The Specialized Truncated Hash</h3><p>SHA-224 is essentially a truncated version of SHA-256. It uses the same mathematical structure and constants as SHA-256 but starts with different initial hash values and discards the final 32 bits of the output. This was intentionally designed by the NSA to provide a security level matching 2-key Triple DES, which was a common requirement for older governmental and financial systems during the transition to modern encryption standards.</p><h3>Security and Collision Resistance</h3><p>From a security perspective, SHA-224 offers a 112-bit security level against collision attacks. While modern systems increasingly favor 128-bit security (found in SHA-256), SHA-224 remains mathematically robust. No practical collision attacks have been discovered against the full SHA-224 algorithm to date. It is considered a secure, "professional-grade" choice for ensuring that message data has not been tampered with during transmission or storage.</p><h3>How SHA-224 Compares to SHA-256</h3><p>The primary difference between the two is the output size. SHA-256 provides a 32-byte hash, while SHA-224 provides 28 bytes. Because they share the same underlying compression function, the calculation speed is nearly identical. Developers often choose SHA-224 when working with database schemas or network protocols that have strict 28-byte limitations but still require the mathematical "fingerprint" reliability and security of the SHA-2 family.</p><h3>Practical Integration and Use Cases</h3><ul><li><strong>Digital Signatures:</strong> Used in specialized certificates to verify the identity of a sender.</li><li><strong>Data De-duplication:</strong> Identifying unique files in storage systems with high efficiency.</li><li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Simulating Git-like object tracking for specific software projects.</li><li><strong>Legacy System Bridge:</strong> Maintaining compatibility with infrastructure designed for 112-bit security standards.</li></ul>'
